**Difference 1 — Storage vs Pipeline:**

```java
// Collection STORES elements:
List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();
trades.add(trade1);  // element stored in internal array
trades.add(trade2);  // element stored
System.out.println(trades.size());  // 2 — knows its size

// Stream STORES NOTHING:
Stream<Trade> stream = trades.stream().filter(Trade::isSettled);
// stream has NO internal array. No elements stored.
// stream has NO size() method.
// It is just a description: "when triggered, pull from trades, apply filter"
```

**Difference 2 — Eager vs Lazy:**

```java
// Collection: ALL elements exist immediately
List<Trade> trades = List.of(trade1, trade2, trade3);  // all 3 in memory NOW

// Stream: elements computed only on demand
Stream<BigDecimal> amounts = trades.stream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)   // NOT executed
    .map(Trade::getAmount);     // NOT executed
// NOTHING has happened yet!
amounts.toList();  // NOW filter+map execute
```

**Difference 3 — Reusable vs Single-use:**

```java
// Collection: iterate unlimited times
for (Trade t : trades) { }  // iteration 1
for (Trade t : trades) { }  // iteration 2 — same data

// Stream: consumed after one terminal
Stream<Trade> s = trades.stream();
s.count();     // consumed
s.toList();    //  IllegalStateException!
```

**Difference 4 — Mutable vs Immutable:**

```java
// Collection: add, remove, set
trades.add(newTrade);
trades.remove(0);
trades.set(1, updatedTrade);

// Stream: cannot modify
// stream.add(newTrade);  // NO such method!
// stream.remove(trade);  // NO such method!
// Stream produces a NEW stream/collection, never modifies source
```

**Difference 5 — External iteration vs Internal iteration:**

```java
// Collection: YOU control iteration (external)
Iterator<Trade> it = trades.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    Trade t = it.next();
    if (t.isSettled()) process(t);
}

// Stream: STREAM controls iteration (internal)
trades.stream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)
    .forEach(this::process);
// You say WHAT, stream decides HOW (sequential, parallel, fused)
```

